{"schema": "workere-benchmark-suite/v1", "protocol": "workere-historical-relations/v1", "config": {"seed": 42, "active_dataset": "causal_timebank", "datasets": {"causal_timebank": {"name": "CausalTimeBank-standard-eci", "repo_id": "Nofing/CausalTimeBank-standard-eci-workere", "split": "train", "text_field": "text", "ann_field": "annots", "max_examples": 0, "kfold": {"enabled": true, "n_folds": 10}, "constrain_to_pair_list": false}}, "data": {"shuffle_docs": true, "shuffle_pair_list": true, "reshuffle_per_resample": true, "binary_undirected": false, "vote_to_binary": false, "binary_default": "norel", "novote_norel": true, "labels": ["causes", "causedby", "norel"]}, "experiment": {"resampling": {"enabled": true, "n_runs": 7, "tie_breaking": "norel", "distinct_fewshots": true}}}, "documents": [{"id": "wsj_0584", "idx": 117, "text": "The bolstered cellular agreement between BellSouth Corp. and LIN Broadcasting Corp. carries heightened risks and could <ei1 fail> to <ei2 fend> off McCaw Cellular Communications Inc., the rival suitor for LIN . Moreover , the amended pact shows how McCaw 's persistence has <ei3 pushed> LIN and BellSouth into a corner , <ei4 forcing> huge debt on the proposed new company . The debt , <ei5 estimated> at $ 4.7 billion , could <ei6 mortgage> the cellular company 's future earning power in order to <ei7 placate> some LIN holders in the short term . The plan still <ei9 calls> for LIN to <ei10 combine> its cellular telephone properties with BellSouth 's and to <ei11 spin> off its broadcasting operations . But under new terms of the agreement , <ei12 announced> Friday , LIN holders would <ei14 receive> a special cash dividend of $ 42 a share , representing a payout of about $ 2.23 billion , shortly before the proposed <ei15 merger> . LIN <ei16 said> it <ei17 expects> to <ei18 borrow> the money to <ei19 pay> the dividend , but <ei20 commitments> from banks still have n't been <ei21 obtained> . Under previous terms , holders would have <ei22 received> a dividend of only $ 20 a share . In addition , New York-based LIN would <ei23 exercise> its right to <ei24 buy> out for $ 1.9 billion the 55 % equity interest of its partner , Metromedia Co., in a New York cellular franchise . That money also would have to be <ei25 borrowed> . In effect , McCaw has <ei26 forced> LIN 's hand by <ei27 bidding> $ 1.9 billion for the stake earlier this month . \" We 're <ei29 taking> on more debt than we would have <ei30 liked> to , \" <ei31 acknowledged> Michael Plouf , LIN 's vice president and treasurer . Although he <ei32 expressed> confidence that the proposed new company 's cash flow would be <ei33 sufficient> to <ei34 cover> interest payments on the debt , he <ei35 estimated> that the company would n't be <ei36 profitable> until 1994 or later . Analyst <ei38 estimate> the value of the BellSouth proposal at about $ 115 to $ 125 a share . They <ei39 value> McCaw 's <ei40 bid> at $ 112 to $ 118 a share . The previous BellSouth pact was <ei41 valued> at about $ 98 to $ 110 a share . McCaw , the largest provider of cellular telephone service in the U.S., already owns about 9.4 % of LIN 's stock . In response to BellSouth 's amended <ei43 pact> , the Kirkland , Wash . , company <ei44 extended> its own <ei45 offer> to <ei46 buy> 22 million LIN shares for $ 125 apiece , which would <ei47 give> McCaw a 50.3 % controlling interest . Over the weekend , McCaw <ei49 continued> to <ei50 call> for an auction of LIN . Analysts <ei51 said> they <ei52 expect> McCaw to <ei53 escalate> the bidding again . \" This game is n't over yet , \" <ei54 said> Joel D. Gross , a vice president at Donaldson , Lufkin amp Jenrette Securities Corp. \" At some point , it will <ei55 become> non-economical for one company . But I do n't <ei56 think> we 're at that point yet . \" Under its revised <ei57 proposal> , Atlanta-based BellSouth would <ei58 have> a 50 % interest in the new cellular company and would be <ei60 responsible> for half of its debt . To <ei61 sweeten> the pact further -- and to <ei62 ease> concerns of institutional investors -- BellSouth <ei63 added> a provision designed to <ei64 give> extra protection to holders if the regional Bell company ever <ei65 decides> to <ei66 buy> the rest of the new cellular company . The provision , described as \" back-end \" protection , would <ei67 require> BellSouth to <ei68 pay> a price equivalent to what an outside party might have to pay . McCaw 's bid also has a similar clause . Only McCaw 's proposal <ei69 requires> the company to <ei70 begin> an auction <ei71 process> in June 1994 for remaining shares at third-party prices . To <ei73 mollify> shareholders <ei74 concerned> about the long-term value of the company under the BellSouth - LIN agreement , BellSouth also <ei75 agreed> to <ei76 pay> as much as $ 10 a share , or $ 540 million , if , after five years , the trading value of the new cellular company is n't as high as the value that shareholders would have realized from the McCaw offer . \" We 're very <ei78 pleased> with the new deal . We did n't <ei79 expect> BellSouth to be so <ei80 responsive> , \" <ei81 said> Frederick A. Moran , president of Moran Asset Management Inc., which holds 500,000 LIN shares . BellSouth 's \" back-end protection was flawed previously . We <ei82 think> this is a superior deal to McCaw 's . We 're surprised . We did n't <ei83 think> a sleeping { Bell } mentality would be <ei84 willing> to <ei85 take> on dilution . \" But Kenneth Leon , a telecommunications analyst with Bear , Stearns amp Co., <ei86 finds> the BellSouth proposal still <ei87 flawed> because the company does n't have to <ei89 wait> five years to begin buying more LIN shares . \" How many shares will be around in 1995 ? \" he asked . \" There 's nothing preventing BellSouth from buying up shares in the meanwhile . \" BellSouth 's revised <ei90 proposal> <ei91 surprised> many industry analysts , especially because of the company 's <ei93 willingness> to <ei94 accept> some dilution of future earnings . William O. McCoy , president of the company 's BellSouth Enterprises Inc. unit , <ei95 said> the revised <ei96 agreement> with LIN would <ei97 dilute> BellSouth earnings by about 9 % in both 1990 and 1991 and by significantly less thereafter . Indeed , BellSouth 's cellular operations were among the first in the country to become profitable . For 1988 , BellSouth <ei101 earned> $ 1.7 billion , or $ 3.51 a share , on revenue of $ 13.6 billion . Analysts were <ei102 predicting> 1990 BellSouth earnings in the range of $ 3.90 a share , or $ 1.9 billion , but now those <ei105 estimates> are being <ei106 scaled> back . In composite <ei107 trading> Friday on the New York Stock Exchange , BellSouth shares <ei109 fell> 87.5 cents to $ 52.125 . In national over-the-counter <ei110 trading> , LIN shares <ei111 soared> $ 4.625 to closed at $ 112.625 , while McCaw <ei112 fell> $ 2.50 a share to $ 37.75 . The proposed BellSouth - LIN cellular company , including the newly acquired Metromedia stake , would <ei113 give> the new entity 55 million potential customers , including about 35 million in the nation 's top 10 markets . Mr. Leon of Bear Stearns <ei114 speculated> that McCaw , in an <ei115 attempt> to <ei116 buy> time , might <ei117 consider> <ei118 filing> an antitrust suit against BellSouth with the Justice Department and U.S. District Judge Harold Greene , who oversees enforcement of the consent decree that <ei119 broke> up the Bell system in 1984 . Indeed , McCaw <ei121 seemed> to <ei122 hint> at that option in a brief <ei123 statement> . Urging LIN directors to conduct \" a fair auction on a level playing field , \" McCaw asked how well the public interest would be served \" with the Bell operating companies controlling over 94 % of all cellular { potential customers } in the nation 's top 10 markets . \"", "gold": [["ei43", "causes", "ei44"], ["ei58", "causes", "ei60"], ["ei89", "causes", "ei87"], ["ei93", "causes", "ei91"], ["ei44", "cause